; Battlezone -- M2: the enemy. See
; docs/battlezone-design.md.
;
;   load "battlezone
;   battlezone
;
; 1/Q drive the left tread, 0/P the
; right, ] fires, space pauses, ESC
; quits.
;
; M1 was the plain, the obstacles, the
; tread controls, the horizon and the
; gunsight -- the milestone that proved
; the PROJECTION is right. M2 adds the
; enemy tank, its hunt logic, both
; shells, the collisions, the explosions
; and the radar, and it is the FRAME
; BUDGET milestone: P11's M2 is the one
; that missed by 19.7 ms, and this is
; the point at which this game finds out
; whether it has the same problem.
;
; WHAT M2 SPENDS, AND WHERE IT GOT THE
; ROOM. M1 measured 50.8 ms a frame at
; 150 MHz and 33.7 at 300 (design
; section 16.4.3), so there are about 16
; ms spare at stock and 33 at the
; overclock. M2's line items, at M0's
; rates: the enemy is 5.3 ms to project
; and 2.4 to draw, the radar about 1,
; the two shells about 2 between them,
; and the collisions about 6. That is
; roughly 17 ms -- which fits at 300 MHz
; with room and does NOT fit at 150.
;
; So THE GAME ASKS FOR THE CLOCK AND
; READS IT BACK. `battlezone` calls
; `hw.setcpu "fast` and then `hw.cpu`,
; and if the board did not take it the
; object cap drops from three to two
; (design section 14's held-in-reserve
; lever, worth 7.3 ms at stock). A board
; played at a density it cannot draw is
; worse than one played at the stock
; density, and the readout says which
; happened.
;
; ONE WRAPPED SCAN A FRAME, AND EVERY
; COLLISION READS ITS TABLE. M1 wrapped
; the obstacle field twice -- once in
; `draw.field` and once in `blocked?` --
; and M2 would have made it five times
; over, since the enemy and both shells
; each need the same eight tests. So the
; wrap is hoisted: `ob.scan` folds all
; eight obstacles into camera-relative
; (obx, obz) once, after the tank's move
; settles, and after that a collision is
; two comparisons rather than a modulo
; pair. It is 3.1 ms that buys back
; about 8, and -- the reason it is
; written this way rather than for the
; milliseconds -- B19 cannot happen,
; because there is exactly one place in
; this file where the plain wraps.
;
; The one consequence to know: the table
; belongs to the camera it was built
; against, so the player's own
; `blocked?` -- which runs BEFORE the
; move commits and therefore before the
; rescan -- tests the step DELTA against
; last frame's table. That is what
; `tk.dx`/`tk.dz` are, and it is exactly
; right rather than an approximation.
;
; The 1980 machine drew vectors on an XY
; monitor from a Math Box that did the
; 3D in hardware. Here the Math Box is
; the Logo interpreter, which is why
; this file is written the way it is
; rather than the way the rest of the
; tree is. What M0 measured (design
; section 12) is that a frame is 44.18 +
; 7.167 n ms on a Pico 2 W and an object
; is 7.2-8.1 ms of it, so every line
; here is cut against a 66.7 ms budget
; at 15 fps with about a dozen
; milliseconds spare.
;
; FOUR THINGS M1 INHERITS SETTLED RATHER
; THAN ASSUMED (design section 16.3.1),
; each of them a decision M0 paid for:
;
; * THE HORIZON IS CULLED FROM THE FIRST
;   LINE. M0 scanned all 32 points on
;   purpose, to price the lever:
;   13.2-13.8 ms, 21 % of a frame, spent
;   on a backdrop. `horizon` never looks
;   at a point it cannot draw -- it
;   works out the first visible INDEX
;   and walks ten.
;
; * THE HOT PATH IS ON GLOBALS, NOT
;   `local` (design section 13, L0.5). A
;   `local` read costs 1.5x a global's,
;   because P11 M4 hashed `find_global`
;   and nothing did the same for the
;   frame walk, and rewriting the
;   projection with globals measured
;   1.31x faster for a bit-identical
;   result -- 3.9 ms a frame. The price
;   is that every hot-path temporary is
;   a global in a language with one flat
;   namespace and no shadowing, which is
;   exactly what cost M0 its whole body
;   column. The mitigation is a PREFIX:
;   `p.` belongs to the projection,
;   `ob.` to the field scan, `mt.` to
;   the horizon and the moon, `tk.` to
;   the tank. The prefix test in
;   test_battlezone.c reads those names
;   back out of this file, so a
;   temporary called `b` fails there
;   rather than silently writing over
;   the camera constant of that name on
;   a board.
;
; * `near` IS CUT FOR EDGE LENGTH, NOT
;   FOR THE SINGULARITY (design section
;   9). An edge costs 0.35 us a step on
;   a Plus 2 W and 0.98 on a Pico 2 W,
;   so drawing cost is proportional to
;   how much screen the edges cover, and
;   what governs that is the near plane:
;   projected size goes as k*h/z, so
;   `near` 8 would put a 40-step cube
;   1,300 steps tall -- four screens --
;   and a dozen of its edges would be
;   most of a frame. At `near` 60 a cube
;   tops out at 173 steps, which is one
;   screen. THE COLLISION RADIUS THEN
;   FOLLOWS THE NEAR PLANE rather than
;   the other way round, so the two can
;   never disagree and you can never get
;   close enough to an obstacle for it
;   to fill the view.
;
; * `max.obstacles` IS A CONSTANT, NOT
;   AN ASSUMPTION. Overclocking (design
;   section 12.3) turns the frame into
;   25.32 + 3.223 n and holds twelve
;   objects at 15 fps where stock holds
;   five, so "three obstacles at 24 fps
;   or twelve at 15" is a real choice --
;   and it is M4's, not this file's.
;
; `window`, AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING
; RATHER THAN TIDINESS. The default is
; `wrap`, which folds a point past the
; edge onto the far side: a horizon
; point at x = -170.8 comes out at
; +149.2 and the segment into it is
; stroked across the whole screen. It
; looks exactly like a projection fault.
; M0's harness was written from the
; design document that says so and still
; missed it. A wireframe that wrapped
; would smear, so this game sets
; `window` at startup and never changes
; it. Lateral clipping needs nothing
; else: `screen_gfx_line` skips
; out-of-bounds pixels one at a time, so
; a line from x = -209 to x = 40 draws
; its visible half and costs the rest
; only in loop iterations.
;
; `splitscreen`, AND IT IS WORTH 8.05
; ms. `screen_gfx_blit_dirty` stops at
; row 240 in split mode, so a
; clear-and-redraw frame presents 240
; rows instead of 320: M0 read 19.2 ms
; against 27.25 full-screen. The bottom
; eight text lines are exactly where the
; cabinet puts its readouts. The
; consequence for the geometry is that
; pixel row is `-y + 160`, so the
; presented band is turtle y in [-79,
; 160] and the optical centre of the
; viewport is y = +40 and not 0 -- which
; is why `hz` below is 40 and every
; screen constant is cut against it.
;
; FOUR RULES CARRIED OVER FROM
; ASTEROIDS, all of them learned the
; hard way:
;
; * A call's last argument absorbs
;   trailing infix, so `item :i :ox -
;   :px` asks for item :i of (:ox -
;   :px). Comparisons here put the
;   constant on the LEFT. Ordinary
;   precedence IS respected -- `a * b -
;   c * d` groups the products -- so the
;   parentheses that are here are the
;   ones that change the answer.
; * The outermost parens of `make "v
;   (...)` are redundant AND cost 15-20
;   % of the statement. They are not
;   written here.
; * A `;` inside a bracketed list
;   silently swallows the rest of a
;   procedure.
; * The per-frame scratch lists are
;   literals set at TOP LEVEL, never
;   inside a procedure: a literal inside
;   a body belongs to the stored
;   procedure and `.setitem` would
;   scribble on the source for good.
;
; A frame ALLOCATES (design section 15)
; -- `.setitem` of a number interns it
; -- so the contract is a flat working
; set rather than a zero, and `reclaim`
; asks the workspace how much room is
; left rather than counting frames.
;
; EVERY LINE HERE FITS 40 COLUMNS, which
; is the width of the PicoCalc's editor.
; That is not free: the wrapped delta in
; `draw.field` is three statements where
; one would fit on a wider screen, and
; the horizon's first index is two. It
; costs about 2 ms a frame and it buys a
; file that can be read on the machine
; it runs on.

;======================================
; Tuning
;
; `k` 260 puts the half-width 160 at
; atan(160/260) = 31.6 degrees, so the
; horizontal field of view is 63
; degrees, close to the cabinet's.

make "fps 15
make "k 260
make "eye 12
make "hz 40

; Nearer than `near` and the object is
; culled WHOLE; further than `far` and
; it is not drawn at all.
make "near 60
make "far 700

; The plain wraps at `world` in both
; axes.
;
; `far` IS LESS THAN `half.world`, and
; that is an invariant rather than a
; taste: an obstacle further than half
; the world in either axis is the same
; obstacle seen the other way round, so
; a cull distance beyond that would draw
; one obstacle twice. The far-plane test
; pins it, because the two are tuned
; separately and would drift apart.
make "world 1600
make "half.world 800

; `coll.r` must exceed `near` +
; half*sqrt2 -- see `blocked?`.
make "coll.r 90

; How many objects a frame will DRAW,
; and how many are in the table (design
; section 14).
;
; TWO CAPS AND A CLOCK. `max.obstacles`
; is what a frame reads; which of the
; two it is set to is decided once, in
; `battlezone`, by asking for the fast
; clock and then READING IT BACK. Three
; objects fit at 300 MHz with the enemy
; and the radar in the frame; at 150
; they do not, and section 14's
; held-in-reserve lever -- three
; obstacles to two -- is worth 7.3 ms,
; which is the difference. M4 turns
; these two numbers; nothing else does.
make "max.obstacles 3
make "fast.obstacles 3
make "slow.obstacles 2
make "ob.count 8

; Obstacle half-width, cube height,
; pyramid height, and the two heights
; above the eye -- hoisted so that the
; per-column statements fit on one line.
make "half 20
make "boxh 40
make "pyrh 56
make "boxe :boxh - :eye
make "pyre :pyrh - :eye

; Degrees a frame per unit of tread
; difference, and turtle steps a frame
; per unit of tread sum.
;
; BOTH ARE PER FRAME, so both are really
; per second times `fps` and neither can
; be read on its own: at 15 fps a pivot
; is two units of difference, so 2 gives
; 60 degrees a second and a one-tread
; arc turns at 30. It was 3 -- 90 a
; second on the pivot -- and a board
; said that is too fast to aim with.
; CHANGE `fps` AND THESE TWO HAVE TO
; MOVE THE OTHER WAY or the tank drives
; and spins faster for no reason a
; player can see.
make "turn.rate 2
make "tread.step 4

make "atom.floor 2000

;--------------------------------------
; The enemy
;
; Design section 8.2. The hull is a box
; that happens to be rotated, so section
; 8.1's column trick still applies --
; with the enemy's own rotation folded
; into the camera's ONCE, rather than
; once a corner -- and the gun is two
; general vertices, because a barrel has
; no ground column. Thirteen edges, and
; that is the shape M0 PRICED: 5.3 ms to
; project and 2.4 to draw on a Pico 2 W.
;
; Section 8.2 also lists a turret box.
; It is not here and it is not an
; oversight: a turret is four more
; columns and twelve more edges, which
; is another 3 ms at stock, and M0
; measured the hull-and-gun form. The
; shape that ships is the shape that was
; measured, exactly as B48 was fixed
; before M0 measured a draw pass. Adding
; it is M4's call with a price attached.
;
; Smaller than an obstacle in both axes,
; so a tank reads as a tank next to a
; cube rather than as a second cube.
make "ehalf 14
make "ebodyh 16
make "ebode :ebodyh - :eye

; Steps and degrees a frame, the
; stand-off it tries to hold, and how
; near straight-on it wants to be before
; it fires.
;
; THE STAND-OFF AND THE HIT BOX ARE ONE
; NUMBER, NOT TWO, and the first board
; play test is what said so: it read as
; a tank that comes straight at you with
; perfect aim. It was. `tk.hit` is a 30
; box, the enemy fires along its own
; heading within `e.aim` of you, so a
; shot thrown sideways by d * tan e.aim
; CANNOT MISS while d * tan 7 < 30 --
; that is everything inside 244 steps,
; and the stand-off was 240. The tank
; drove into the one place from which it
; could not miss and stopped there.
;
; So the stand-off is the number that
; moved: 400, which is 283 even on the
; diagonal where `e.d`'s Manhattan
; distance is furthest from the real
; one, and 283 * tan 10 is 50 against a
; 30 box. `e.wob` below is the other
; half of the same fix.
make "e.step 6
make "e.turn 4
make "e.range 400
make "e.aim 7
make "e.naim 0 - :e.aim

; The aim error, in degrees either way,
; and the span `random` needs to produce
; it.
;
; THE STAND-OFF ALONE WOULD NOT HAVE
; FIXED IT. `hunt` turns in `e.turn`
; steps and stops the moment it is
; inside `e.aim`, so the error it fires
; with is whatever that last 4-degree
; step left -- and against a player who
; is holding still it is the SAME error
; every shot. The tank does not miss
; sometimes and hit sometimes; it hits
; every time or misses every time, for
; the whole approach. That is not an
; aim, it is a coin flipped once.
;
; A per-shot error is what the cabinet's
; tank had and it costs two `sin`/`cos`
; calls A SHOT rather than a frame,
; which at the measured 20 ms body is
; not a number worth thinking about.
make "e.wob 10
make "e.wob2 21

; Frames between hunt decisions (design
; section 19.3: at 15 fps a decision
; every third frame is invisible and
; costs a third as much) and frames
; between its shots.
make "e.think 3
make "e.reload 24

; Where a new one appears, and the two
; collision half-widths: what a shell
; has to come within to kill it, and
; what it has to come within to be
; stopped by an obstacle.
make "e.spawn 620
make "e.hit 34
make "e.coll 60

;--------------------------------------
; The shells
;
; One each, as the cabinet has. A shell
; moves `sh.step` a frame, so its
; collision guards have to be at least
; half a step wider than the thing they
; test against or it tunnels straight
; through at speed: 20 + 16 for an
; obstacle, 14 + 16 for the enemy tank,
; and the same again for the shell that
; is coming the other way.
make "sh.step 32
make "sh.frames 22
make "sh.guard 36
make "tk.hit 30

;--------------------------------------
; The explosion
;
; Screen-space fragments from the hit
; point, as Asteroids' `draw.boom` is,
; and for the same reason: drifting the
; wreck in three dimensions would want a
; projection a fragment. Five short
; strokes on a radius that grows with
; the death frame reads as a tank coming
; apart and costs fifteen statements on
; ten frames out of every enemy's life.
make "boom.frames 10
make "boom.drift 7

;--------------------------------------
; The radar
;
; Design section 11: an `arc`, a swept
; wedge and a blip. It sits above the
; mountains and clear of the moon --
; which is why `moon.y` came down to 100
; when this arrived.
;
; THE BLIP NEEDS NO TRIGONOMETRY AT ALL,
; and that is the whole reason the radar
; is cheap. A radar is a top-down view
; in the CAMERA's frame, and the camera
; frame coordinates of the enemy are
; exactly the (xc, zc) its projection
; already computes. Scale them and add
; the centre: three statements, no
; bearing, no arctangent, no distance.
make "rd.x 0
make "rd.y 140
make "rd.r 19
; The face of the radar is the far
; plane: rd.r / far, so an enemy you
; could draw is an enemy you can see on
; the glass.
make "rd.sc 0.027
make "rd.spin 24
make "rd.fov 32
make "rd.sw 0

; Green as the cabinet's vectors were,
; red as its overlay band was.
make "world.colour 249
make "moon.colour 254
make "sight.colour 248

;======================================
; The world
;
; Eight obstacles on a plain that wraps.
; Positions are kept in [0, world) and
; every delta is wrapped, because B19 is
; the precedent here: Asteroids got the
; picture right and the COMPARISON wrong
; and shipped that way for a milestone.
; The wrap-arithmetic test is written
; against that and not against the
; drawing.
;
; None is within 200 steps of the start
; point, so the first frame is a drive
; and not a bump.
;
; Kind 1 is a cube, kind 2 is a pyramid.
; The arcade field is both.
make "ox [260 620 1180 140]
make "ox se :ox [900 1460 700 1220]
make "oz [1180 300 980 540]
make "oz se :oz [1420 660 1050 1500]
make "okind [1 2 1 1]
make "okind se :okind [2 1 2 1]

; The camera. (px, pz, ph), the eye 12
; steps above the plain, no pitch and no
; roll -- a tank stays flat, which
; removes an entire axis from every
; transform and is not a simplification
; of the original.
;
; Heading 0 looks down +z and increases
; clockwise, which is Logo's own
; compass, so sin/cos fall out of it
; with no sign fixes: forward is (px +
; s*sn, pz + s*cs) and the camera
; transform is
;
;   xc = dx*cs - dz*sn
;   zc = dz*cs + dx*sn
make "px 800
make "pz 800
make "ph 0

; cos and sin of the heading, hoisted
; ONCE A FRAME -- the file's most
; important hoist, and the difference
; between two trig calls a frame and two
; per vertex. `a` and `b` are the
; obstacle half-offsets already rotated,
; hoisted once a frame for every box in
; the world.
make "cs 1
make "sn 0
make "a 0
make "b 0

; The wrap offsets, hoisted after the
; tank moves: a delta is `modulo (ox +
; ob.u) world` less half the world, and
; hoisting the camera out of it is what
; keeps the scan to three statements an
; axis.
make "ob.u 0
make "ob.v 0

; Four ground columns: screen x, and the
; two screen heights that share the
; divide. Mutated in place -- a list
; built per object per frame would mint
; twelve cells a frame and put `recycle`
; inside the loop.
make "cx [0 0 0 0]
make "cy1 [0 0 0 0]
make "cy2 [0 0 0 0]

; The pyramid's apex, which a cube does
; not have and which costs one extra
; divide.
make "apx 0
make "apy 0

make "left.tread 0
make "right.tread 0
make "bumped false
make "paused false
make "quit false
make "frame.count 0
make "ob.n 0

; The obstacle field, folded once a
; frame into camera-relative offsets.
; Mutated in place; see `ob.scan`.
make "obx [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0]
make "obz [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0]

; The enemy. World position and heading,
; its rotation constants, its offsets
; from the camera in world axes and in
; the camera's frame, and the three
; intents `hunt` leaves behind for the
; two frames in which it does not run.
make "e.x 800
make "e.z 1400
make "e.h 180
make "e.alive true
make "e.cool 0
make "e.boom 0
make "e.ec -1
make "e.es 0
make "e.dx 0
make "e.dz 0
make "e.xc 0
make "e.zc 0
make "e.t 0
make "e.f 0
make "e.fire false

; The player's shell and the enemy's.
; World position, the velocity fixed at
; the moment of firing -- a shell flies
; the heading it was fired along, not
; the one you are pointing now -- and
; the offsets from the camera.
make "sh.on false
make "sh.x 0
make "sh.z 0
make "sh.vx 0
make "sh.vz 0
make "sh.dx 0
make "sh.dz 0
make "sh.life 0
make "es.on false
make "es.x 0
make "es.z 0
make "es.vx 0
make "es.vz 0
make "es.dx 0
make "es.dz 0
make "es.life 0

; The explosion, in screen space, and
; the pause a hit costs the player.
make "bm.x 0
make "bm.y 0
make "bm.n 0
make "tk.boom 0

; What the readout tallies besides the
; milliseconds.
make "kills 0
make "hits 0

; What a frame cost. `body.ms` is the
; Logo work; `frame.ms` is the whole
; period, `sync` included. See
; `play.frame` for why both are needed
; and neither alone will do.
make "body.ms 0
make "frame.ms 0
;
; The readout's accumulators: how many
; frames since the last one, and the sum
; and peak of each figure over them. The
; clock is read once through a `catch`,
; because a board with no settable clock
; has no `hw.cpu` at all and a number
; nobody can attribute to a clock is
; worth nothing -- `normal` is 150 MHz
; and `fast` is 300, and the frame is
; 1.6x cheaper at the second.
make "hud.n 0
make "hud.bs 0
make "hud.bm 0
make "hud.fs 0
make "hud.fm 0
make "hud.a 0
make "cpu.at "unknown
make "cpu.was "unknown

to cam.setup
  make "cs cos :ph
  make "sn sin :ph
  make "a :half * :cs
  make "b :half * :sn
end

; Hoisted separately from `cam.setup`,
; because the heading is settled before
; the tank moves and the position is
; settled after it.
to cam.offsets
  make "ob.u :half.world - :px
  make "ob.v :half.world - :pz
end

; THE ONLY PLACE IN THIS FILE WHERE THE
; PLAIN WRAPS. Every obstacle in the
; table is folded once into an offset
; from the camera in world axes, and
; after that the field scan, the tank's
; collisions, the enemy's and both
; shells' are two comparisons apiece
; against a number that is already
; small.
;
; Eight statements an obstacle -- 3.9 ms
; at stock, and the 40-column rule owns
; two of them, since `.setitem` of
; `:ob.d - :half.world` is 46 characters
; on one line. It replaces the six
; `draw.field` did per obstacle and the
; six `blocked?` did per CALLER, and by
; M2 there are four callers. With one it
; would not have been worth it.
;
; It runs AFTER the tank's move commits,
; so the table belongs to the camera the
; frame will be drawn from. The one
; caller that cannot wait for it is the
; tank's own `blocked?`, which has to
; run before the move it is deciding --
; and that is why `blocked?` takes an
; offset rather than a position.
to ob.scan
  repeat :ob.count [
    make "ob.d :ob.u + item repcount :ox
    make "ob.d modulo :ob.d :world
    make "ob.d :ob.d - :half.world
    .setitem repcount :obx :ob.d
    make "ob.d :ob.v + item repcount :oz
    make "ob.d modulo :ob.d :world
    make "ob.d :ob.d - :half.world
    .setitem repcount :obz :ob.d
  ]
end

;======================================
; Projection
;
; Design section 8.1: an obstacle is
; axis-aligned and stands on the plain,
; so its eight vertices are four ground
; COLUMNS lifted to two heights. With
; `a` and `b` hoisted once a frame for
; every obstacle in the world, the four
; corners are eight ADDS and no
; multiplies, and the top and bottom of
; a column share one divide. That is the
; trick the whole budget rests on: eight
; vertices for the cost of four
; transforms.
;
; UNROLLED on purpose. A `repeat` over
; the four would need `repcount` and an
; `item` walk per corner to find its
; offsets, and this is the loop the
; frame is linear in. It is also what a
; person would write.
;
; `p.dx` and `p.dz` are INPUTS AND ARE
; GLOBALS, not parameters. A parameter
; is a local, a local read costs 1.5x a
; global's (section 13 L0.5), and the
; whole point of this milestone's naming
; discipline is that the hot path never
; touches the frame walk.
;
; Outputs `true` if the object is
; drawable and `false` if ANY column is
; inside the near plane -- any, not all:
; one corner behind you swings the
; projection through infinity and throws
; a line across the whole screen
; (section 9). It is wrong in exactly
; one visible way, that an obstacle you
; are pressed against vanishes rather
; than filling the view, and `blocked?`
; is what makes that unreachable.
to project.box
  make "p.xc :p.dx * :cs - :p.dz * :sn
  make "p.zc :p.dz * :cs + :p.dx * :sn
  make "p.z1 :p.zc + :a + :b
  make "p.z2 :p.zc - :a + :b
  make "p.z3 :p.zc - :a - :b
  make "p.z4 :p.zc + :a - :b
  if :near > :p.z1 [output "false]
  if :near > :p.z2 [output "false]
  if :near > :p.z3 [output "false]
  if :near > :p.z4 [output "false]
  make "p.xa :p.xc + :a
  make "p.xb :p.xc - :a
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z1
  .setitem 1 :cx (:p.xa - :b) * :p.iz
  .setitem 1 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  .setitem 1 :cy2 :hz + :boxe * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z2
  .setitem 2 :cx (:p.xa + :b) * :p.iz
  .setitem 2 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  .setitem 2 :cy2 :hz + :boxe * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z3
  .setitem 3 :cx (:p.xb + :b) * :p.iz
  .setitem 3 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  .setitem 3 :cy2 :hz + :boxe * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z4
  .setitem 4 :cx (:p.xb - :b) * :p.iz
  .setitem 4 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  .setitem 4 :cy2 :hz + :boxe * :p.iz
  output "true
end

; A pyramid is the same four ground
; columns with the top ring collapsed to
; a point, and the apex is the object's
; OWN CENTRE COLUMN lifted -- so it
; costs one extra divide and nothing
; else (section 8.1).
;
; The column half repeats `project.box`,
; and the repetition is deliberate:
; folding the two together would mean
; either four extra `item` reads to
; recover the shared reciprocals or a
; branch inside the loop the whole
; budget is linear in. The
; projections-agree test is what keeps
; the copy honest.
to project.pyr
  make "p.xc :p.dx * :cs - :p.dz * :sn
  make "p.zc :p.dz * :cs + :p.dx * :sn
  make "p.z1 :p.zc + :a + :b
  make "p.z2 :p.zc - :a + :b
  make "p.z3 :p.zc - :a - :b
  make "p.z4 :p.zc + :a - :b
  if :near > :p.z1 [output "false]
  if :near > :p.z2 [output "false]
  if :near > :p.z3 [output "false]
  if :near > :p.z4 [output "false]
  make "p.xa :p.xc + :a
  make "p.xb :p.xc - :a
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z1
  .setitem 1 :cx (:p.xa - :b) * :p.iz
  .setitem 1 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z2
  .setitem 2 :cx (:p.xa + :b) * :p.iz
  .setitem 2 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z3
  .setitem 3 :cx (:p.xb + :b) * :p.iz
  .setitem 3 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z4
  .setitem 4 :cx (:p.xb - :b) * :p.iz
  .setitem 4 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.zc
  make "apx :p.xc * :p.iz
  make "apy :hz + :pyre * :p.iz
  output "true
end

;======================================
; Drawing
;
; `clean` and redraw, for the reason P11
; section 3.3 measured: a scattered
; vector scene dirties most tile ROWS
; either way, because the dirty tracker
; keeps one inclusive span per row, so
; an erase pass buys almost nothing and
; costs a second traversal. A horizon
; line spans the full width by itself,
; which settles it before the argument
; starts.
;
; An edge is `(setpos x y)` with the pen
; down -- ONE statement, one straight
; line, NOTHING ALLOCATED. That spelling
; is B48's fix (2026-08-23); before it
; the choices were `setpos list :x :y`,
; which mints two cons cells a call and
; so 140 a frame, or `setx`/`sety`,
; which draws an L.
;
; Twelve edges in TWENTY statements, and
; the four the design first missed are
; the pen-ups the verticals need: a
; vertical does not start where the last
; one ended. The twelve-edge test pins
; the count.
;
; ONE CONSEQUENCE WORTH KNOWING BEFORE A
; FIELD IS LAID OUT: an edge's cost is
; proportional to its length ON SCREEN,
; so a near object costs more to draw
; than a far one and the frame gets MORE
; expensive as the player closes on
; something. That is the opposite of
; what a culling budget usually does.
to draw.box
  pu (setpos item 1 :cx item 1 :cy1)
  pd (setpos item 2 :cx item 2 :cy1)
  (setpos item 3 :cx item 3 :cy1)
  (setpos item 4 :cx item 4 :cy1)
  (setpos item 1 :cx item 1 :cy1)
  pu (setpos item 1 :cx item 1 :cy2)
  pd (setpos item 2 :cx item 2 :cy2)
  (setpos item 3 :cx item 3 :cy2)
  (setpos item 4 :cx item 4 :cy2)
  (setpos item 1 :cx item 1 :cy2)
  pu (setpos item 1 :cx item 1 :cy1)
  pd (setpos item 1 :cx item 1 :cy2)
  pu (setpos item 2 :cx item 2 :cy1)
  pd (setpos item 2 :cx item 2 :cy2)
  pu (setpos item 3 :cx item 3 :cy1)
  pd (setpos item 3 :cx item 3 :cy2)
  pu (setpos item 4 :cx item 4 :cy1)
  pd (setpos item 4 :cx item 4 :cy2)
  pu
end

; Eight edges in eleven statements: the
; base quad, then corners 1 and 2 to the
; apex in one chain and corners 3 and 4
; in a second. A pyramid is the cheaper
; obstacle at both ends -- one extra
; divide to project and two thirds of a
; cube's edges to draw -- which is why
; the field carries three of them.
to draw.pyr
  pu (setpos item 1 :cx item 1 :cy1)
  pd (setpos item 2 :cx item 2 :cy1)
  (setpos item 3 :cx item 3 :cy1)
  (setpos item 4 :cx item 4 :cy1)
  (setpos item 1 :cx item 1 :cy1)
  (setpos :apx :apy)
  (setpos item 2 :cx item 2 :cy1)
  pu (setpos item 3 :cx item 3 :cy1)
  pd (setpos :apx :apy)
  (setpos item 4 :cx item 4 :cy1)
  pu
end

;======================================
; The obstacle field
;
; The wrap is `ob.scan`'s, done once for
; the whole frame, so this reads a
; camera-relative offset straight out of
; the table -- two statements an
; obstacle rather than six. That is M2's
; change here and the only one.
;
; The reject is a DIAMOND and not a
; disc: `abs dx + abs dz` is one
; statement where a squared distance is
; three, and it is stricter than the
; disc, so an obstacle at 45 degrees
; pops in at 0.7 of `far`. Objects
; popping in at the far cull distance is
; design section 14's accepted cut
; rather than a defect.
;
; `max.obstacles` caps the objects a
; frame DRAWS, and it is spent on the
; far side of the near cull rather than
; the near side of it. The far cull is a
; distance test, so an obstacle BEHIND
; you survives it and only `project.box`
; rejects it -- and spending the budget
; before that rejection lets the
; obstacles behind you crowd out the one
; in front. That is not an edge case
; here: about three of the eight survive
; the far cull on a typical frame and
; the cap is three, so it binds nearly
; every frame and roughly half of what
; it would be spent on is behind the
; camera. What it looks like from the
; driving seat is a cube dead ahead that
; simply never appears.
;
; The rejected projection is still paid
; for, and that is fine: it early-outs
; after six statements and the table is
; only eight entries long, so the
; unbounded half of the work is bounded
; by `ob.count` anyway. What the cap
; protects is the drawing, which is
; where the milliseconds are.
;
; A cap of zero therefore draws nothing,
; which is how M4 turns the density down
; without editing this procedure. `stop`
; inside `repeat` leaves the enclosing
; procedure, which is what ends the
; scan.
to draw.field
  make "ob.n 0
  repeat :ob.count [
    make "p.dx item repcount :obx
    make "p.dz item repcount :obz
    make "ob.d (abs :p.dx) + (abs :p.dz)
    if :far > :ob.d [
      if :ob.n = :max.obstacles [stop]
      make "ob.k item repcount :okind
      ifelse 1 = :ob.k [
        if project.box [
          draw.box
          make "ob.n :ob.n + 1
        ]
      ] [
        if project.pyr [
          draw.pyr
          make "ob.n :ob.n + 1
        ]
      ]
    ]
  ]
end

;======================================
; The horizon
;
; The mountain range, the volcano and
; the moon are at INFINITY: screen x is
; k * tan(azimuth - heading) and screen
; y comes straight from a stored table.
; No divide, no rotation, and no
; dependence on where you are -- they
; scroll with your heading and never
; with your position, which is the only
; way to tell that you are turning on an
; empty plain.
;
; IT IS A TANGENT AND NOT A SCALE, AND
; THAT IS WORTH A TANGENT A POINT. In a
; pure pivot DISTANCE DOES NOT MATTER: a
; rotation changes every bearing by the
; same amount, so a peak at infinity and
; a cube in front of you, at the same
; place on screen, have to move by the
; same number of pixels. Distance
; cancels out of k * xc/zc entirely.
;
; This used to map the azimuth linearly
; -- (azimuth - heading) * 5.06, section
; 8.4's saving -- and a straight line
; against a tangent is not the same
; curve twice. The mountains scrolled at
; a CONSTANT rate while the world
; accelerated toward the edges: 11.4 %
; too fast at the centre of the view,
; right at about 20 degrees off it, and
; 13.9 % too SLOW near the edge. Over a
; second of turning a cube and a peak
; that started together finished 32
; steps apart, and the drift reversed
; sign across the screen. It was found
; by watching it turn, which is the only
; way it shows -- every still frame is
; plausible.
;
; The cost is one statement and one
; `tan` a point, about 0.6 ms, and it
; buys the horizon back into the same
; projection as everything else. The
; increment trick goes with it: the
; spacing is no longer uniform, so each
; point takes the tangent of its own
; running angle. That angle is never
; wrapped and never leaves -45..+45
; degrees, so the tangent is bounded by
; 1 and the singularity is unreachable.
;
; THE CULL IS THE DESIGN AND NOT AN
; OPTIMISATION. M0 scanned all 32 points
; to price it and read 13.2-13.8 ms --
; 21 % of a frame, the largest line item
; after the present -- because four
; statements a point at 48.5-53.5 us is
; what four statements a point costs. So
; this never touches a point it cannot
; draw: it works out the first visible
; INDEX and walks ten from there.
;
; Forty points and not thirty-two.
; Section 8.4's arithmetic is that the
; view is 63 degrees wide, so a 32-point
; table shows about five peaks and a
; 40-point one about seven -- and seven
; is already coarse for a mountain
; range. The cull is what makes the
; density affordable: scanned whole,
; forty points would be 17 ms and
; absurd.
;
; THE INDEX IS WALKED IN SCREEN ORDER,
; WHICH IS WHY THERE IS NO SEAM. M0's
; harness scanned the table in index
; order and drew in screen order, and at
; heading 0 that steps from +171 to -171
; in one segment and strokes a line
; clear across the view. Here the
; running azimuth is never wrapped at
; all -- only the table INDEX is, with
; `modulo` -- so consecutive points are
; always adjacent on screen and the
; polyline is one unbroken run.
;
; The +360 in `mn.off` is not
; decoration: `int` truncates toward
; zero rather than flooring, so a
; heading under 36 degrees would give an
; index one too high and leave a gap at
; the left edge. Shifted, the argument
; is always positive and `int` is a
; floor.
;
; The table is a silhouette you can read
; off the numbers. Indices 14 to 19
; (azimuth 117 to 162 degrees) are the
; volcano: a broad rise to 46, a crater
; notch at 40, a second lip at 52, and
; back down. Everything else is range.
make "mtn [6 11 5 14 8 18 9]
make "mtn se :mtn [4 12 7 16 6 10 22]
make "mtn se :mtn [34 46 40 52 30 15 8]
make "mtn se :mtn [13 5 17 9 20 7 11]
make "mtn se :mtn [15 6 19 8 13 5 21]
make "mtn se :mtn [10 16 7 12 4]

; Points in the table and degrees
; between them.
make "mn.n 40
make "mn.step 9

; Degrees of margin either side of
; straight ahead, and the shift that
; makes `int` a floor.
make "mn.arc 36
make "mn.off 360 - :mn.arc

; Points AFTER the first, and this one
; is derived rather than cut by hand.
; The first point lands somewhere in
; [-(arc + step), -arc] degrees, so
; nine more 9-degree steps carry it to
; [+36, +45] -- and since the view ends
; at 31.6 degrees, BOTH ends are off
; screen at every heading with room to
; spare. What actually holds it is the
; horizon-coverage test: change
; `mn.step` or `mn.arc` and the test
; re-cuts this number by failing.
make "mn.seen 9

; THE GROUND, AND IT IS ONE LINE.
;
; The plain is at `eye` below the camera
; and screen y is `hz - eye/z * k`, so
; as z goes to infinity the ground rises
; to exactly `hz` and stops. That is the
; true horizon: the line where the plain
; meets the sky, at the same y whatever
; the heading and wherever you are.
;
; It is here because without it the
; mountain range reads as a squiggle
; hanging in space rather than as a
; ridge standing on something -- a
; wireframe silhouette is a LINE and not
; a filled shape, so nothing anchors its
; lower edge -- and because it is the
; only thing below the peaks that tells
; the driver where the ground is.
; Design section 14 cuts ground texture
; on the grounds that the cabinet has
; none, which is true and is exactly why
; the cabinet's own horizon has to do
; this job alone.
;
; SEPARATE FROM `horizon` ON PURPOSE.
; This segment spans the whole screen
; deliberately, and a horizon segment
; that spans the whole screen is the
; signature of the `wrap` defect M0 hit
; -- so the test that forbids one still
; watches `horizon` alone and this is
; not an exception carved into it.
;
; A full-width edge is the most
; expensive single line the game draws:
; about 0.38 ms of pixel loop on a Pico
; 2 W at section 10's measured 0.98 us a
; step, 0.18 on a Plus 2 W, plus three
; statements. It buys the whole scene a
; floor.
to ground
  pu (setpos -160 :hz)
  pd (setpos 160 :hz)
  pu
end

to horizon
  make "mt.i :ph + :mn.off
  make "mt.i int :mt.i / :mn.step
  make "mt.a :mt.i * :mn.step - :ph
  make "mt.a :mt.a - 360
  make "mt.x :k * tan :mt.a
  make "mt.k (modulo :mt.i :mn.n) + 1
  pu
  (setpos :mt.x :hz + item :mt.k :mtn)
  pd
  repeat :mn.seen [
    make "mt.i :mt.i + 1
    make "mt.a :mt.a + :mn.step
    make "mt.x :k * tan :mt.a
    make "mt.k (modulo :mt.i :mn.n) + 1
    (setpos :mt.x :hz + item :mt.k :mtn)
  ]
  pu
end

; The moon is a crescent: two arcs of
; the same radius whose centres are ten
; steps apart, which is the shape you
; get by taking one disc out of another.
; `arc` is centred on the turtle and
; does not move it, so each arc is a
; `setpos`, a `seth` and one C-side call
; -- the headings and sweeps are the
; intersection geometry for r 16 and d
; 10, worked out once here rather than
; per frame.
;
; It is skipped when it is off the side,
; which is most of the time, and that
; test is three statements -- the same
; wrap the horizon deliberately avoids,
; but here once a frame rather than once
; a point. `arc` segments at one chord
; per 4 degrees whatever the radius, so
; a crescent is 90 short device-side
; lines however small it is drawn: a
; fixed charge, paid on roughly one
; frame in five, and one of the numbers
; the play test should read off the HUD
; rather than take from this comment.
make "moon.az 300
make "moon.y 100
make "moon.r 16
make "moon.d 10

; The bearing beyond which it cannot be
; on screen, and the cull HAS to be in
; degrees rather than in steps now: the
; moon can be anywhere in the circle,
; and `tan` of a bearing near 90 degrees
; is not a number you want to hand to
; `setpos`. At 38 degrees the crescent's
; right edge is already off the right of
; the view, so nothing is lost.
make "moon.arc 38

to moon
  make "mt.m :moon.az - :ph + 180
  make "mt.m (modulo :mt.m 360) - 180
  if :moon.arc > abs :mt.m [
    make "mt.m :k * tan :mt.m
    draw.moon
  ]
end

to draw.moon
  make "mt.m2 :mt.m + :moon.d
  setpc :moon.colour
  pu (setpos :mt.m :moon.y)
  seth 162
  pd arc 216 :moon.r
  pu (setpos :mt.m2 :moon.y)
  seth 199
  pd arc 143 :moon.r
  pu
  setpc :world.colour
end

;======================================
; The gunsight
;
; A fixed overlay with NO ARITHMETIC IN
; IT AT ALL, exactly as P11 section 6.1
; writes an outline: eight edges in
; sixteen statements, every one of them
; a literal, in the cabinet's red rather
; than the world's green. Drawn after
; the whole plain so that it sits over
; anything the world put in the middle
; of the view.
;
; TWO BRACKETS FACING EACH OTHER, with a
; stalk running outward from each. The
; top bar sits 30 above the aiming point
; with its ends turning DOWN toward it;
; the bottom bar 30 below with its ends
; turning UP; and the two stalks leave
; along the centreline, outward, in the
; one direction nothing else occupies.
;
; EVERY NUMBER IS MIRRORED ABOUT y = 40,
; and that is the thing to preserve if
; this is redrawn: bars at +/-30, teeth
; reaching back in to +/-15, stalks from
; +/-30 out to +/-60, and +/-30 of width
; either side of the centreline. A sight
; that is not symmetric about its aiming
; point tells you the gun is somewhere
; it is not.
;
; NOTHING IN IT SHARES A ROW WITH THE
; HORIZON, AND THE GROUND LINE IS WHY.
; This sight used to be two long arms at
; y = 40 with the verticals hanging off
; them -- and 40 is `hz`, so the arms
; lay exactly along the ground line
; section 8.3a added. Two things drawn
; on the same row of pixels in two
; colours are one thing as far as the
; eye is concerned: the sight read as
; part of the horizon and the horizon
; read as part of the sight, and neither
; could be seen for what it was.
;
; The bars are horizontal again, and
; that was never the problem. What
; matters is not the direction of a
; stroke but that no stroke lies on `hz`
; or crosses it, which is what the test
; checks and why it has no opinion about
; the shape.
;
; THE GAP IS THE POINT OF THE SHAPE. The
; aiming point is (0, 40) -- a shell
; flies at eye height, so it appears at
; y = `hz`, dead centre when it is aimed
; -- which is on the horizon and is
; exactly where the target is. So the
; middle thirty rows are left empty: the
; target sits in clear air, inside a
; frame that says where the gun is
; pointing without ever covering what it
; is pointing at.
to gunsight
  setpc :sight.colour
  pu (setpos 0 70) pd (setpos 0 100)
  pu (setpos 0 10) pd (setpos 0 -20)
  pu (setpos -30 70) pd (setpos 30 70)
  pu (setpos -30 10) pd (setpos 30 10)
  pu (setpos -30 70) pd (setpos -30 55)
  pu (setpos 30 70) pd (setpos 30 55)
  pu (setpos -30 10) pd (setpos -30 25)
  pu (setpos 30 10) pd (setpos 30 25)
  pu
end

;======================================
; The tank
;
; Motion is PER TREAD. `left.tread` and
; `right.tread` are each -1, 0 or +1,
; the pair drives forward speed (l + r)
; and turn rate (l - r), and that is the
; cabinet's dual-stick control. THE SIGN
; IS THE PHYSICAL ONE AND IT READS
; BACKWARDS: a tank whose RIGHT tread
; runs forward pivots to the LEFT, so a
; clockwise turn -- an increasing Logo
; heading -- needs l > r. Design section
; 7 said (r - l) until M1 drove it.
;
; ONE KEY PER TREAD PER DIRECTION, which
; is the cabinet's two sticks as closely
; as a keyboard can hold them: 1 runs
; the LEFT tread forward and Q runs it
; back, 0 runs the RIGHT tread forward
; and P runs it back. The keys are where
; the sticks are -- 1 and Q on the left
; of the keyboard, 0 and P on the right
; -- so each hand drives its own side
; and the player never converts an
; intention into a direction. Driving
; straight is both forward keys, a pivot
; is one of each, and a one-tread arc is
; one key: nine tread pairs, four keys,
; no mapping in between.
;
; The arrows drove it until now through
; a forward intent and a turn intent
; summed into the pair, which is a
; steering wheel wearing a tank's
; controls. Design section 7 always said
; the motion is per tread; this is the
; input finally saying the same thing,
; and it is what retires the M4 question
; of whether a direct key pair feels
; better.
;
; Both keys of one tread at once is the
; back one, the same way down beat up
; before: the second `if` simply runs
; last. Nothing has to clamp, because no
; tread is ever the sum of two keys --
; `clamp1` went with the arrows.
to treads
  make "left.tread 0
  if keydown? 49 [make "left.tread 1]
  if keydown? 113 [make "left.tread -1]
  make "right.tread 0
  if keydown? 48 [make "right.tread 1]
  if keydown? 112 [make "right.tread -1]
end

; The collision radius follows the near
; plane (section 9) rather than the
; other way round: the near cull drops
; an object when ANY of its columns
; comes inside `near`, and the nearest
; column of an axis-aligned cube sits at
; most half*sqrt2 = 28.3 steps in front
; of its centre, so a centre-distance
; guard of near + 28.3 fires before the
; cull ever can. `coll.r` 90 is that
; number rounded up. The
; collision-radius test is what stops
; the two drifting apart.
;
; A square test rather than a round one,
; because it is one comparison an axis
; and an obstacle IS square.
;
; FOUR CALLERS BY M2 AND ONE PREDICATE.
; The tank's move, the enemy's move, and
; each shell's flight all ask the same
; question of the same eight obstacles,
; so this takes the two things that
; differ -- an offset from the camera in
; `tk.dx`/`tk.dz` and a half-width in
; `tk.guard` -- through globals and
; leaves the scan alone. B19 is the
; precedent for having ONE of these: the
; bug there was one collision test out
; of several getting the wrap wrong, and
; a single predicate over a single
; wrapped table cannot have that.
;
; It reads `ob.scan`'s table, so it
; tests an OFFSET and not a position,
; and the offset is from the camera the
; table was built against. For the
; enemy and the shells that camera is
; this frame's, because they step after
; the rescan. For the TANK it is last
; frame's, because a move has to be
; refused before it is committed and the
; rescan follows the commit -- so the
; tank passes the step delta itself,
; which is exactly right rather than an
; approximation of it.
;
; The z read is inside the x test, so an
; obstacle that is nowhere near costs
; two statements rather than three.
to blocked?
  repeat :ob.count [
    make "ob.x item repcount :obx
    make "ob.x :ob.x - :tk.dx
    if :tk.guard > abs :ob.x [
      make "ob.z item repcount :obz
      make "ob.z :ob.z - :tk.dz
      if :tk.guard > abs :ob.z [
        output "true
      ]
    ]
  ]
  output "false
end

to move.tank
  make "tk.dx :tk.s * :sn
  make "tk.dz :tk.s * :cs
  make "tk.guard :coll.r
  ifelse blocked? [make "bumped true] [
    make "tk.x :px + :tk.dx
    make "px modulo :tk.x :world
    make "tk.z :pz + :tk.dz
    make "pz modulo :tk.z :world
  ]
end

; Turn, then hoist the camera constants,
; then move -- in that order, so the
; step is taken along the heading the
; frame will be drawn from and
; `cam.setup`'s sin/cos pair serves
; both. Two trig calls a frame, and the
; alternative is four.
;
; A blocked move is refused outright and
; the turn is kept, which is the
; arcade's behaviour: you can always
; turn away from what you have run into.
;
; The wrap offsets are hoisted last,
; after the position is final, because
; `draw.field` reads them once an
; obstacle.
to step.tank
  if :tk.boom > 0 [
    make "tk.boom :tk.boom - 1
    make "left.tread 0
    make "right.tread 0
    make "bumped false
    stop
  ]
  treads
  make "tk.r :left.tread - :right.tread
  make "ph :ph + :tk.r * :turn.rate
  make "ph modulo :ph 360
  cam.setup
  make "tk.s :left.tread + :right.tread
  make "tk.s :tk.s * :tread.step
  make "bumped false
  if not 0 = :tk.s [move.tank]
  cam.offsets
  ob.scan
end


;======================================
; The enemy
;
; Design section 8.2. THE HULL IS A BOX
; THAT HAPPENS TO BE ROTATED, so section
; 8.1's four-columns trick still applies
; -- with the enemy's own rotation
; folded into the camera's ONCE a frame
; rather than once a corner.
;
; The transform is linear, so the
; transform of a corner is the centre's
; transform plus the offset's, and the
; four corners are then plus-and-minus
; sums exactly as an obstacle's are.
;
; AND THE SECOND OFFSET IS FREE. The
; enemy's forward half-offset is
; ehalf*(sin eh, cos eh); its right one
; is that turned 90 degrees, and turning
; 90 degrees COMMUTES with the camera's
; rotation. So the camera-frame right
; offset is (p.pz, -p.px) read straight
; off the forward one, and two
; multiplies and two adds a frame go
; away. M0's harness spent them; it was
; measuring cost and not direction.
;
; M0's harness also had the gun pointing
; at 90 - eh rather than eh, because it
; built its half-offset from (cos, sin)
; instead of (sin, cos). With a fixed
; heading and nothing aiming down the
; barrel that is invisible, which is
; exactly why it survived a measurement
; run. It is not invisible here: `hunt`
; aims the gun and `enemy.fires` shoots
; along it.
;
; THE GUN TIP CAN BE BEHIND THE NEAR
; PLANE WHILE THE HULL IS NOT, and it is
; still safe to divide by. The tip is
; the centre plus two forward offsets,
; so it reaches at most 2*ehalf = 28
; steps past the centre; every corner is
; at least `near` away and no corner is
; more than ehalf*sqrt2 = 19.8 in front
; of the centre, so the centre is beyond
; 79.8 and the tip beyond 51.8. Near,
; never zero, and never negative.
make "gunx [0 0]
make "guny [0 0]

to project.enemy
  make "p.fx :ehalf * :e.es
  make "p.fz :ehalf * :e.ec
  make "p.px :p.fx * :cs - :p.fz * :sn
  make "p.pz :p.fz * :cs + :p.fx * :sn
  make "p.za :e.zc + :p.pz
  make "p.zb :e.zc - :p.pz
  make "p.z1 :p.za - :p.px
  make "p.z2 :p.za + :p.px
  make "p.z3 :p.zb + :p.px
  make "p.z4 :p.zb - :p.px
  if :near > :p.z1 [output "false]
  if :near > :p.z2 [output "false]
  if :near > :p.z3 [output "false]
  if :near > :p.z4 [output "false]
  make "p.xa :e.xc + :p.px
  make "p.xb :e.xc - :p.px
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z1
  .setitem 1 :cx (:p.xa + :p.pz) * :p.iz
  .setitem 1 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  .setitem 1 :cy2 :hz + :ebode * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z2
  .setitem 2 :cx (:p.xa - :p.pz) * :p.iz
  .setitem 2 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  .setitem 2 :cy2 :hz + :ebode * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z3
  .setitem 3 :cx (:p.xb - :p.pz) * :p.iz
  .setitem 3 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  .setitem 3 :cy2 :hz + :ebode * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.z4
  .setitem 4 :cx (:p.xb + :p.pz) * :p.iz
  .setitem 4 :cy1 :hz - :eye * :p.iz
  .setitem 4 :cy2 :hz + :ebode * :p.iz
  make "p.iz :k / :e.zc
  .setitem 1 :gunx :e.xc * :p.iz
  .setitem 1 :guny :hz + :ebode * :p.iz
  make "p.g :e.zc + :p.pz + :p.pz
  make "p.iz :k / :p.g
  make "p.g :e.xc + :p.px + :p.px
  .setitem 2 :gunx :p.g * :p.iz
  .setitem 2 :guny :hz + :ebode * :p.iz
  output "true
end

; Thirteen edges: the hull's twelve and
; the gun's one. `draw.box` reads the
; same three column lists the obstacles
; do, which is why the enemy is drawn in
; its own phase rather than inside the
; field scan.
to draw.enemy
  draw.box
  pu (setpos item 1 :gunx item 1 :guny)
  pd (setpos item 2 :gunx item 2 :guny)
  pu
end

; The enemy's offset from the camera in
; WORLD axes, wrapped -- the same fold
; `ob.scan` does, once, for one object.
; Everything else about the enemy is
; derived from these two numbers: the
; hunt reads them, the collisions read
; them, and the camera-frame pair the
; projection and the radar want is two
; more statements on top.
to enemy.offsets
  make "e.d :ob.u + :e.x
  make "e.d modulo :e.d :world
  make "e.dx :e.d - :half.world
  make "e.d :ob.v + :e.z
  make "e.d modulo :e.d :world
  make "e.dz :e.d - :half.world
end

; The same offset in the CAMERA's frame,
; which is what the projection needs and
; -- because a radar is a top-down view
; in exactly that frame -- what the blip
; needs too. Taken after the enemy's
; move rather than with the world-axis
; pair, so both readers see where it
; ended up and not where it started.
to enemy.camera
  make "e.xc :e.dx * :cs - :e.dz * :sn
  make "e.zc :e.dz * :cs + :e.dx * :sn
end

; Design section 19.3: at 15 fps a
; decision every third frame is
; invisible and costs a third as much,
; so this runs on one frame in
; `e.think` and leaves three INTENTS
; behind -- turn, drive and fire -- that
; the other two frames act on. That is
; the difference between a hunt that
; costs 0.6 ms a frame and one that
; costs 0.2.
;
; The bearing is one `arctan` rather
; than a cross product: the two-input
; form takes the signs of both inputs,
; so it is defined all the way round and
; through zero, which the one-input form
; is not. Player minus enemy is minus
; the offset, hence the +180.
;
; The range test is the field scan's
; DIAMOND and not a disc, for the same
; reason it is there: one statement
; against three, and an enemy that
; closes to 0.7 of the stand-off on the
; diagonal is an enemy that presses a
; little harder from the corners.
to hunt
  make "e.b (arctan :e.dz :e.dx) + 180
  make "e.b :e.b - :e.h + 180
  make "e.b (modulo :e.b 360) - 180
  make "e.t 0
  if :e.b > :e.aim [make "e.t 1]
  if :e.naim > :e.b [make "e.t -1]
  make "e.d (abs :e.dx) + (abs :e.dz)
  make "e.f 1
  if :e.range > :e.d [make "e.f 0]
  make "e.fire false
  if 1 > :e.cool [
    if :e.aim > abs :e.b [
      if :far > :e.d [make "e.fire true]
    ]
  ]
end

; A blocked enemy is refused its step
; and keeps its turn, exactly as the
; player is, so it grinds around a cube
; rather than through it. It does not
; try to go round -- an enemy stuck
; behind an obstacle is the cabinet's
; behaviour and it is what makes hiding
; behind one work.
to move.enemy
  make "e.vx :e.step * :e.es
  make "e.vz :e.step * :e.ec
  make "tk.dx :e.dx + :e.vx
  make "tk.dz :e.dz + :e.vz
  make "tk.guard :e.coll
  if blocked? [stop]
  make "e.d :e.x + :e.vx
  make "e.x modulo :e.d :world
  make "e.d :e.z + :e.vz
  make "e.z modulo :e.d :world
  make "e.dx :tk.dx
  make "e.dz :tk.dz
end

to enemy.fires
  make "e.fire false
  if :es.on [stop]
  make "e.cool :e.reload
  make "es.on true
  make "es.x :e.x
  make "es.z :e.z
  make "e.d :e.h + (random :e.wob2)
  make "e.d :e.d - :e.wob
  make "es.vx :sh.step * sin :e.d
  make "es.vz :sh.step * cos :e.d
  make "es.dx :e.dx
  make "es.dz :e.dz
  make "es.life :sh.frames
end

; A new one appears at `e.spawn` on a
; random bearing, already facing you --
; the cabinet gives you that courtesy
; too, and then starts closing.
;
; AND NOT INSIDE A CUBE. `blocked?`
; keeps the tank and the enemy out of an
; obstacle by refusing the move that
; would take them in, which means
; neither can ever be inside one -- with
; exactly one exception, a spawn, which
; arrives somewhere rather than driving
; there. An enemy that starts inside the
; guard radius can never leave it, in
; any direction, because every candidate
; step is still inside: it sits in the
; middle of a cube shooting at you for
; the rest of its life. So a spawn is
; TESTED, and re-rolled up to four
; times. Four is not a guarantee and
; does not need to be -- eight obstacles
; on a 1,600-step plain make one bad
; bearing unlikely and four in a row
; negligible, and the fallback is the
; behaviour this replaced.
to spawn.enemy
  repeat 4 [
    place.enemy
    make "tk.dx :e.dx
    make "tk.dz :e.dz
    make "tk.guard :e.coll
    if not blocked? [stop]
  ]
end

to place.enemy
  make "e.b random 360
  make "e.d :e.spawn * sin :e.b
  make "e.d :px + :e.d
  make "e.x modulo :e.d :world
  make "e.d :e.spawn * cos :e.b
  make "e.d :pz + :e.d
  make "e.z modulo :e.d :world
  make "e.h :e.b + 180
  make "e.h modulo :e.h 360
  make "e.ec cos :e.h
  make "e.es sin :e.h
  make "e.alive true
  make "e.cool :e.reload
  make "e.t 0
  make "e.f 1
  make "e.fire false
  enemy.offsets
  enemy.camera
end

; The turn is taken before the move, and
; the camera-frame pair after it, so the
; projection and the radar both read the
; place the enemy actually ended up.
to step.enemy
  if :e.boom > 0 [
    make "e.boom :e.boom - 1
    if 1 > :e.boom [spawn.enemy]
    stop
  ]
  enemy.offsets
  make "e.d modulo :frame.count :e.think
  if 0 = :e.d [hunt]
  make "e.h :e.h + :e.t * :e.turn
  make "e.h modulo :e.h 360
  make "e.ec cos :e.h
  make "e.es sin :e.h
  if :e.cool > 0 [
    make "e.cool :e.cool - 1
  ]
  if not 0 = :e.f [move.enemy]
  enemy.camera
  if :e.fire [enemy.fires]
end

;======================================
; The shells
;
; One each, as the cabinet has, and the
; two are written out twice rather than
; folded into one procedure over a
; two-element list. That is the same
; call `project.box` and `project.pyr`
; make: an `item` walk to find which
; shell this is would cost more
; statements than the duplication saves,
; in the loop the frame is linear in,
; and the two differ in what they hit
; anyway.
;
; A shell keeps its VELOCITY from the
; moment it was fired rather than its
; heading -- it flies where the gun was
; pointing, not where the gun is now --
; and it keeps a world position, because
; it outlives several of the camera's
; own steps and the plain wraps
; underneath it.
;
; THE ENEMY'S SHELL HITS THE PLAYER WITH
; TWO COMPARISONS AND NO ARITHMETIC AT
; ALL, because the player is at the
; origin of the camera-relative frame
; every offset in this file is already
; in. That is the cheapest collision in
; the game and it is the one that
; matters most.
;
; The guards are half a step wider than
; the things they test against, or a
; 32-step shell walks straight through a
; 20-step cube between two frames. That
; is the whole reason `sh.guard` is 36
; and not 20.

to fire
  if :sh.on [stop]
  if :tk.boom > 0 [stop]
  make "sh.on true
  make "sh.x :px
  make "sh.z :pz
  make "sh.vx :sh.step * :sn
  make "sh.vz :sh.step * :cs
  make "sh.dx 0
  make "sh.dz 0
  make "sh.life :sh.frames
end

to step.shell
  if not :sh.on [stop]
  make "sh.life :sh.life - 1
  if 1 > :sh.life [
    make "sh.on false
    stop
  ]
  make "sh.d :sh.x + :sh.vx
  make "sh.x modulo :sh.d :world
  make "sh.d :sh.z + :sh.vz
  make "sh.z modulo :sh.d :world
  make "sh.d :ob.u + :sh.x
  make "sh.d modulo :sh.d :world
  make "sh.dx :sh.d - :half.world
  make "sh.d :ob.v + :sh.z
  make "sh.d modulo :sh.d :world
  make "sh.dz :sh.d - :half.world
  if :e.alive [
    make "sh.d :sh.dx - :e.dx
    if :e.hit > abs :sh.d [
      make "sh.d :sh.dz - :e.dz
      if :e.hit > abs :sh.d [
        kill.enemy
        stop
      ]
    ]
  ]
  make "tk.dx :sh.dx
  make "tk.dz :sh.dz
  make "tk.guard :sh.guard
  if blocked? [make "sh.on false]
end

to step.eshell
  if not :es.on [stop]
  make "es.life :es.life - 1
  if 1 > :es.life [
    make "es.on false
    stop
  ]
  make "es.d :es.x + :es.vx
  make "es.x modulo :es.d :world
  make "es.d :es.z + :es.vz
  make "es.z modulo :es.d :world
  make "es.d :ob.u + :es.x
  make "es.d modulo :es.d :world
  make "es.dx :es.d - :half.world
  make "es.d :ob.v + :es.z
  make "es.d modulo :es.d :world
  make "es.dz :es.d - :half.world
  if :tk.hit > abs :es.dx [
    if :tk.hit > abs :es.dz [
      hit.player
      stop
    ]
  ]
  make "tk.dx :es.dx
  make "tk.dz :es.dz
  make "tk.guard :sh.guard
  if blocked? [make "es.on false]
end

; A shell is a four-pixel dash and not a
; projected object: it has no size worth
; transforming and one edge is the
; smallest thing this engine can draw.
; The player's flies at EYE HEIGHT, so
; its screen y is `hz` with no
; arithmetic at all -- which is also why
; a shot you aimed goes through the
; middle of the gunsight.
to draw.shell
  make "p.zc :sh.dz * :cs + :sh.dx * :sn
  if :near > :p.zc [stop]
  make "p.xc :sh.dx * :cs - :sh.dz * :sn
  make "p.iz :k / :p.zc
  make "p.sx :p.xc * :p.iz
  make "p.s2 :p.sx + 4
  pu (setpos :p.sx :hz)
  pd (setpos :p.s2 :hz)
  pu
end

to draw.eshell
  make "p.zc :es.dz * :cs + :es.dx * :sn
  if :near > :p.zc [stop]
  make "p.xc :es.dx * :cs - :es.dz * :sn
  make "p.iz :k / :p.zc
  make "p.sx :p.xc * :p.iz
  make "p.sy :hz + :ebode * :p.iz
  make "p.s2 :p.sx + 4
  pu (setpos :p.sx :p.sy)
  pd (setpos :p.s2 :p.sy)
  pu
end

;======================================
; The explosion
;
; Asteroids' `draw.boom` in screen
; space: five short strokes on a radius
; that grows with the death frame. The
; alternative is drifting the wreck in
; three dimensions, which wants a
; projection per fragment for ten frames
; and buys a difference nobody watching
; an explosion can see.
;
; `boom.frag` takes its bearing as a
; PARAMETER, which the rest of this file
; does not do. A local read costs 1.5x a
; global's and that is worth 3.9 ms in
; the frame walk (design section 13,
; L0.5); this runs on ten frames of an
; enemy's whole life, so the five copies
; the global form would need cost more
; to read than the locals cost to run.
;
; The projection is done ONCE, at the
; moment of the kill, and the fragments
; then live in screen space. A tank that
; blew up 300 steps away does not get
; closer while it burns.
to kill.enemy
  make "sh.on false
  make "es.on false
  make "e.alive false
  make "e.boom :boom.frames
  make "kills :kills + 1
  make "bm.n 0
  if :near > :e.zc [stop]
  make "p.iz :k / :e.zc
  make "bm.x :e.xc * :p.iz
  make "bm.y :hz + :ebode * :p.iz
  make "bm.n :boom.frames
end

; Being hit costs the player the pause
; and nothing else yet: lives, the score
; and the cracked screen are M3. The
; explosion is at the centre of the view
; because that is where the shell was
; when it arrived.
to hit.player
  make "es.on false
  make "hits :hits + 1
  make "tk.boom :boom.frames
  make "bm.x 0
  make "bm.y :hz
  make "bm.n :boom.frames
end

to boom.frag :d
  pu (setpos :bm.x :bm.y)
  seth :d
  fd :bm.t
  pd fd 8
  pu
end

to draw.boom
  if 1 > :bm.n [stop]
  make "bm.n :bm.n - 1
  make "bm.t :boom.frames - :bm.n
  make "bm.t :bm.t * :boom.drift
  setpc :moon.colour
  boom.frag 20
  boom.frag 85
  boom.frag 150
  boom.frag 215
  boom.frag 290
  setpc :world.colour
end

;======================================
; The radar
;
; Design section 11: a circle, the field
; of view as a fixed wedge, a sweep line
; that turns, and a blip.
;
; THE BLIP NEEDS NO TRIGONOMETRY AND NO
; BEARING. A radar is a top-down view in
; the CAMERA's frame, and the enemy's
; camera-frame coordinates are exactly
; the (e.xc, e.zc) `step.enemy` already
; computed for the projection. Scale
; them, add the centre, and that is the
; blip -- three statements, against the
; arctangent and the distance the
; obvious spelling wants.
;
; The wedge is fixed rather than swept
; with the heading, because on a radar
; that is drawn in the camera's frame
; "ahead" is always up. The sweep line
; turns because the cabinet's did; it
; is decoration and it costs four
; statements.
;
; Drawn last, over everything, in the
; overlay's red -- it is a piece of
; glass in front of the periscope and
; not a thing on the plain.
to blip
  make "rd.bx :e.xc * :rd.sc
  make "rd.by :e.zc * :rd.sc
  make "rd.d (abs :rd.bx) + (abs :rd.by)
  if :rd.r > :rd.d [
    make "rd.bx :rd.x + :rd.bx
    make "rd.by :rd.y + :rd.by
    pu (setpos :rd.bx :rd.by)
    seth 0
    bk 2
    pd fd 4
    pu
  ]
end

to radar
  setpc :sight.colour
  make "rd.sw :rd.sw + :rd.spin
  make "rd.sw modulo :rd.sw 360
  pu (setpos :rd.x :rd.y)
  pd arc 360 :rd.r
  seth :rd.sw
  fd :rd.r
  pu (setpos :rd.x :rd.y)
  seth :rd.fov
  pd fd :rd.r
  pu (setpos :rd.x :rd.y)
  seth 0 - :rd.fov
  pd fd :rd.r
  pu
  if :e.alive [blip]
  setpc :world.colour
end

;======================================
; Input
;
; `pollkeys` reads the keyboard's STATE
; -- which keys are down right now --
; and is called once at the top of the
; frame, after which `keydown?` and
; `keyhit?` cost nothing. LEVEL FOR THE
; CONTROLS THAT HOLD, EDGE FOR THE ONES
; THAT FIRE: the treads are read with
; `keydown?` in `treads`, pause and quit
; with `keyhit?` here, or holding space
; would toggle the pause fifteen times a
; second.
;
; This runs OUTSIDE the paused guard, or
; a paused game could never read the key
; that unpauses it, so it turns the
; other keys away itself while paused --
; the defect both shipped shooters had.
to poll.input
  pollkeys
  if keyhit? 32 [
    make "paused not :paused
    stop
  ]
  if :paused [stop]
  if keyhit? 177 [make "quit true]
  if keyhit? 93 [fire]
end

;======================================
; The HUD
;
; M1's readout is a development one and
; M3 replaces it with the score: what
; the first hardware play test has to
; answer is whether driving FEELS like
; Battlezone at this frame rate, and
; that question needs the frame time on
; the screen beside the driving.
;
; TWO NUMBERS, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF
; THIS SHOWED ONLY THE WRONG ONE. `sync`
; does not just wait -- it PRESENTS and
; then waits -- so a figure measured up
; to `sync` leaves out the present, and
; the present is 19.8 ms of a 66.7 ms
; budget on every board M0 measured. A
; board read "low to mid 20s" against a
; predicted 46.5 and the prediction was
; fine: 46.5 less the 19.8 nobody was
; counting is 26.7, and the readout was
; answering a different question from
; the one it was being compared against.
; So:
;
; * BODY is the Logo work -- everything
;   from the top of the frame to just
;   before `sync`. That is what the
;   game's own levers move.
;
; * FRAME is the whole period, top of
;   one frame to the top of the next,
;   `sync` included. It is what says
;   whether the rate is being made: at
;   15 fps it reads 66 or 67 while there
;   is headroom and climbs above it when
;   there is not.
;
; The present itself is FRAME less BODY
; less the wait, and Logo cannot
; separate those two -- `sync` is one
; primitive. M0 measured it at 19.8 ms
; and flat, so it is a constant to
; subtract rather than a number to
; watch.
;
; AVERAGED OVER A SECOND, because a
; figure that changes fifteen times a
; second cannot be read off a screen by
; somebody driving. Each frame adds to a
; sum and a peak; every `hud.every`
; frames the averages are printed and
; the accumulators reset. The peak is
; the half that matters for the budget
; -- it is where a `recycle` spike shows
; -- and the average is the half you can
; read.
;
; It is all inside the measured body, so
; the readout pays for itself in its own
; numbers, and it lags one frame because
; it tallies the figures the previous
; frame left behind. At 15 fps neither
; is perceptible.
;
; Rows 24 to 27: with B49 fixed the
; split screen shows text lines 24 to
; 31, so this sits directly under the
; viewport. M2's row is the tallies --
; kills and hits are what a play test
; reads to know whether the enemy is
; playing the game or standing still.

make "hud.every 15

to hud.tally
  if 2 > :frame.count [stop]
  make "hud.n :hud.n + 1
  make "hud.bs :hud.bs + :body.ms
  make "hud.fs :hud.fs + :frame.ms
  if :body.ms > :hud.bm [
    make "hud.bm :body.ms
  ]
  if :frame.ms > :hud.fm [
    make "hud.fm :frame.ms
  ]
  if :hud.n = :hud.every [draw.hud]
end

to draw.hud
  setcursor [0 24]
  print (se [POS] int :px int :pz)
  setcursor [14 24]
  print (se [HDG] int :ph)
  setcursor [26 24]
  print (se [CPU] :cpu.at)
  make "hud.a int :hud.bs / :hud.n
  setcursor [0 25]
  print (se [BODY] :hud.a)
  setcursor [14 25]
  print (se [MAX] :hud.bm)
  make "hud.a int :hud.fs / :hud.n
  setcursor [0 26]
  print (se [FRAME] :hud.a)
  setcursor [14 26]
  print (se [MAX] :hud.fm)
  setcursor [0 27]
  print (se [KILLS] :kills)
  setcursor [14 27]
  print (se [HITS] :hits)
  make "hud.n 0
  make "hud.bs 0
  make "hud.fs 0
  make "hud.bm 0
  make "hud.fm 0
end

;======================================
; The frame
;
; NOTHING IN THIS INTERPRETER COLLECTS
; ON DEMAND: `alloc_cell` and `mem_atom`
; report out of space rather than
; collecting and retrying, which is the
; right call for a frame loop -- no
; unpredictable pause -- but it makes
; this the game's entire safety margin.
; Asteroids learned that counting frames
; is always a guess and was wrong three
; times before it asked the workspace
; instead, so this asks from the start.
; A recycle is about 4.3 ms when it
; fires.
to reclaim
  if atoms < :atom.floor [recycle]
end

; One frame, in a fixed order, as a
; procedure rather than the body of the
; loop below so that a test or a timing
; script can call exactly what the game
; runs.
;
; The order is: read the world, then
; clear, then draw far to near --
; ground, horizon, moon, obstacles,
; enemy, shells, explosion -- and then
; the two pieces of glass, the gunsight
; and the radar, over everything.
; `clean` takes the graphics half only,
; so the readout in the text area
; survives it and never needs erasing.
;
; EVERY MOVE HAPPENS BEFORE ANY DRAW,
; and that is not tidiness. The tank
; moves first because `ob.scan` follows
; its move and everything after that
; reads the table; the enemy moves
; second because both shells test
; themselves against where it ended up;
; and the shells move last because the
; frame that kills the enemy must not
; also draw it. A shell stepped after
; the drawing would hit a tank the
; player had already seen explode.
;
; THE TWO TIMERS BRACKET DIFFERENT
; THINGS ON PURPOSE. `body.ms` stops
; before `sync` and is the Logo work;
; `frame.ms` stops after it and is the
; whole period. `sync` PRESENTS and then
; waits, so the first excludes the
; present and the second includes it
; along with whatever slack is left --
; which is why a readout showing only
; the first cannot be compared against
; 66.7 ms, and why the first version of
; this file was wrong to try.
;
; `t0` is taken before `poll.input` and
; outside the paused guard, so the
; period is the real one and a paused
; frame still measures itself.
;
; `hud.tally` and `reclaim` are both
; inside the body: the readout because
; it should pay for itself in its own
; numbers, and the recycle because a 4.3
; ms spike is exactly what a play test
; wants to see rather than have smoothed
; away.
to play.frame
  make "tk.t0 ticks
  poll.input
  if not :paused [
    make "frame.count :frame.count + 1
    step.tank
    step.enemy
    step.shell
    step.eshell
    clean
    setpc :world.colour
    ground
    horizon
    moon
    draw.field
    if :e.alive [
      if project.enemy [draw.enemy]
    ]
    if :sh.on [draw.shell]
    if :es.on [draw.eshell]
    draw.boom
    gunsight
    radar
    hud.tally
    reclaim
    make "body.ms ticks - :tk.t0
  ]
  sync
  make "frame.ms ticks - :tk.t0
end

;======================================
; The game
;
; `cs` restores automatic refresh, so it
; comes BEFORE the `setrefresh "sync`
; and not after -- Asteroids logged that
; one the hard way. `window` comes after
; `cs` for the same reason: `cs` puts
; the boundary mode back to `wrap`.
;
; ASK FOR THE CLOCK, THEN READ IT BACK,
; AND CUT THE DENSITY IF THE BOARD SAID
; NO. M2's line items are about 17 ms
; and there are 16 spare at 150 MHz and
; 33 at 300 (design section 16.4.3), so
; the overclock is the enabling
; condition for this milestone and not a
; luxury. `hw.cpu` reads the HARDWARE
; rather than remembering what was
; asked, so a board that refused cannot
; report an overclock that bought
; nothing -- and a board with no
; settable clock at all has no `hw.cpu`
; either, which is what the `catch` is
; for. Either way the answer lands on
; the readout beside the milliseconds it
; explains.
;
; The cut is design section 14's
; held-in-reserve lever, three obstacles
; to two, and it is 7.3 ms at stock. A
; board played at a density it cannot
; draw is worse than one played at the
; stock density.
to clock
  make "cpu.was "unknown
  catch "error [make "cpu.was hw.cpu]
  make "cpu.at :cpu.was
  catch "error [hw.setcpu "fast]
  catch "error [make "cpu.at hw.cpu]
  make "max.obstacles :fast.obstacles
  if not "fast = :cpu.at [
    make "max.obstacles :slow.obstacles
  ]
end

; AND GIVE IT BACK ON THE WAY OUT. A
; game that leaves the board overclocked
; has changed the machine and not just
; played on it: everything after it runs
; at a clock nobody asked for. On a
; board with PSRAM that used to be worse
; than impolite -- the QMI's timing for
; the external RAM is computed once, at
; boot, against the clock running then,
; and nothing retuned it, so the editor
; came back corrupted (B50, fixed in the
; device layer). The clock is given back
; here anyway: a game should leave the
; machine as it found it.
;
; It restores WHAT IT FOUND rather than
; `normal`, so a session that was
; already fast when the game started
; stays fast when it ends. `cpu.was` is
; `unknown` on a board with no settable
; clock, and there is nothing to put
; back there.
to restore.clock
  if "unknown = :cpu.was [stop]
  if :cpu.was = :cpu.at [stop]
  catch "error [hw.setcpu :cpu.was]
end

to battlezone
  cs
  splitscreen
  ct
  window
  ht
  clock
  make "px 800
  make "pz 800
  make "ph 0
  cam.setup
  cam.offsets
  ob.scan
  make "sh.on false
  make "es.on false
  make "bm.n 0
  make "tk.boom 0
  make "e.boom 0
  make "kills 0
  make "hits 0
  make "rd.sw 0
  spawn.enemy
  make "paused false
  make "quit false
  make "frame.count 0
  make "hud.n 0
  make "hud.bs 0
  make "hud.fs 0
  make "hud.bm 0
  make "hud.fm 0
  (setrefresh "sync :fps)
  ; A baseline poll, so the keypress
  ; that started the game is not
  ; delivered to the first frame as a
  ; hit.
  pollkeys
  until [:quit] [play.frame]
  setrefresh "auto
  restore.clock
  textscreen
  ct
  print [Battlezone M2]
end
